  <h2>Why hands-on learning for professionals?</h2>

  <p>
    You don&rsquo;t need to be a data scientist to understand how LLMs work. But
    if you&rsquo;re making decisions about AI tools, leading teams that use
    them, or trying to cut through vendor hype, having a concrete mental model
    matters.
  </p>

  <p>
    This workshop gives you that model through direct experience. You&rsquo;ll
    build a simple language model by hand&mdash;counting patterns in text,
    rolling dice to generate new text, seeing where it works and where it fails.
    No programming required.
  </p>

  <p>
    The result? You&rsquo;ll understand what&rsquo;s actually happening when you
    type a prompt into ChatGPT, why these tools sometimes produce nonsense, and
    what the fundamental limitations are. You&rsquo;ll be able to have informed
    conversations about AI adoption and help your team use these tools more
    effectively.
  </p>

  <h2>Suggested ways in</h2>

  <p>
    Every organisation is different, so which <a href="/lessons/">lessons</a> you
    choose to learn will depend on your specific needs and goals. We suggest you start
    with the <strong>Fundamentals</strong>, and then choose-your-own adventure
    from there.
  </p>

  <p>A few more facilitation tips:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>mixed technical groups</strong>: pair technical and non-technical
      participants for the training step&mdash;different perspectives often
      surface useful insights
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>sceptics</strong>: the hands-on format works well for sceptics
      because it&rsquo;s concrete&mdash;you&rsquo;re not asking anyone to
      believe claims about AI, you&rsquo;re showing them how patterns in text
      become generated output
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>follow-up</strong>: after the workshop, participants often want to
      explore the <a href="/lessons/">full lesson library</a>&mdash;send them
      the link and let them go deeper on topics that interest them
    </li>
  </ul>
</PageLayout>
